ndahl  hershman index  which you don't have to know about  anymore but it basically means  uh it sets a number uh above which  uh a certain level uh uh you know a  market is highly concentrated  how you define a market sometimes comes  into play with these  and and uh at the at the level  of enforcement uh sometimes that that  line is drawn very tightly  uh to say well if you're talking about  local  search and this local area uh then maybe  that's not  tightly concentrated but if you look  sort of with your eyes in reality then  yes obviously a market is concentrated  um you know there used to be  much uh more uh precise standards around  this that  uh there were market share thresholds  where  uh if if the justice department saw that  a company owned more or controlled more  than 30  of a market or 35 or some number uh that  they would step in  and move to break up that that that  industry or or or deny a merger for  those reasons  uh we don't have that as much today this  performed all hirschmann is very  technical  uh as i say you can draw the line in  various different ways and so  um you know i prefer to look at this  sort of in  on an experiential basis if you go out  in the world  and uh you know you go into your  supermarket aisle and you see the same  uh it looks like a lot of uh different  choices but a lot of them are made by  the same companies  uh if you you know try to rent a car go  on a flight or  or you know do the things that you do  throughout your day  you find that there's more and more  concentration within  those sectors and those service  providers i think  a lot of people if asked what's the  concern  about monopoly power overwhelmingly  people would say well if few firms  control a market they can drive up the  price that i pay  for a good or a service but it goes well  beyond that doesn't it  yes it does uh i mean it doesn't go  beyond that in terms of how the justice  department and the federal trade  commission look at things that they 